  "before_interfaces": {
    "lem-qsm-dt-finite-trace-pairing-cyclicity": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $d,e\\geq1$. For $A\\in M_{e\\times d}(\\mathbb C)$ and $B\\in M_{d\\times e}(\\mathbb C)$,\n$$ \\operatorname{Tr}_e(AB)=\\operatorname{Tr}_d(BA), $$\nwhere $\\operatorname{Tr}_r$ is the diagonal-sum trace on $M_r(\\mathbb C)$. This includes cyclicity for square matrices. For $C\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, the bilinear trace pairing is nondegenerate: if $\\operatorname{Tr}_d(XC)=0$ for every $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, then $C=0$. No adjoint is inserted in this pairing."
    ],
    "def-qsm-dt-channel-definition": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nLet $d,e\\geq1$ be integers, let $H_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\mathbb C^d$ and $H_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\mathbb C^e$ with their standard inner products, and write $M_d(\\mathbb C)$ and $M_e(\\mathbb C)$ for the complex linear maps on these spaces. A matrix $X$ is **positive semidefinite**, written $X\\geq0$, when it is Hermitian and $\\langle Xv,v\\rangle\\geq0$ for every vector $v$. The finite spectral convention in [[lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus]] gives positive matrices their orthonormal eigenbases and positive square roots.\n\nA **positive map** is a complex-linear map $\\Phi:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_e(\\mathbb C)$ such that $\\Phi(X)\\geq0$ whenever $X\\geq0$. For each integer $r\\geq1$, identify $M_r(\\mathbb C)\\otimes M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with matrices on $\\mathbb C^r\\otimes\\mathbb C^d$ in the ordered product basis. The map $\\Phi$ is **completely positive** when $\\operatorname{id}_r\\otimes\\Phi$ is positive for every such $r$, where $\\operatorname{id}_r$ is the identity map on $M_r(\\mathbb C)$.\n\nThe map $\\Phi$ is **trace-preserving** when $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi(X))=\\operatorname{Tr}(X)$ for every $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$. A **finite quantum channel** is a complex-linear map that is both completely positive and trace-preserving, abbreviated CPTP. Unitality, $\\Phi(I_d)=I_e$, is a separate condition and is not part of this definition.\n\nThis is a mathematical definition. A physical preparation, measurement, or open-system model must separately identify when one of these maps represents a physical transformation."
    ],
    "def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nFix a finite dimension $d\\geq1$, a positive constant $\\hbar$ in joule-seconds, a Hermitian matrix $H\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ in joules, and a finite family $L_1,\\ldots,L_m\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with units ${\\rm s}^{-1/2}$, where $m\\geq0$. On dimensionless matrices $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, define the **finite Gorini–Kossakowski–Sudarshan–Lindblad (GKSL) generator**\n\n$$\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_{a=1}^{m}\\left(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\}\\right),$$\n\nwhere $[A,B]=AB-BA$, $\\{A,B\\}=AB+BA$, and $A^*$ is the conjugate transpose. Each summand has units ${\\rm s}^{-1}$ times the units of $X$. An empty sum when $m=0$ is zero. The resulting $\\mathcal L$ is a linear map on the matrix space used in the finite-channel definition [[def-qsm-dt-channel-definition]].\n\nFor a compact time interval $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ whose coordinate is time in seconds, continuous functions $H:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ and $L_a:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with $H(t)=H(t)^*$ define the corresponding **continuous finite GKSL family** $\\mathcal L_t$ by the same formula at each $t$. A propagator for this family means a family of linear maps $\\Lambda(t,s)$, $s\\leq t$ in $I$, satisfying $\\partial_t\\Lambda(t,s)=\\mathcal L_t\\circ\\Lambda(t,s)$ and $\\Lambda(s,s)=\\operatorname{id}$. This definition alone asserts neither existence nor uniqueness.\n\nThese are finite-dimensional mathematical definitions. They do not assert that a physical system follows this generator or that a microscopic bath has a Markovian limit."
    ],
    "thm-qsm-dt-choi-kraus": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $d,e\\geq1$ and let $\\Phi:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_e(\\mathbb C)$ be complex-linear. Choose the standard input basis $(|i\\rangle)_{i=1}^d$, let $E_{ij}=|i\\rangle\\langle j|$, and use the ordered tensor convention input-reference $\\otimes$ output. Define the (unnormalized) Choi matrix\n\n$$J(\\Phi):=\\sum_{i,j=1}^{d}E_{ij}\\otimes\\Phi(E_{ij})\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)\\otimes M_e(\\mathbb C).$$ Then $\\Phi$ is completely positive if and only if $J(\\Phi)\\geq0$, and this holds if and only if there are $q\\leq de$ matrices $K_1,\\ldots,K_q:\\mathbb C^d\\to\\mathbb C^e$ such that $$\\Phi(X)=\\sum_{a=1}^{q}K_aXK_a^*\\qquad(X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)).$$\n\nThe empty sum is allowed when $\\Phi=0$. For any such Kraus representation, $\\Phi$ is trace-preserving if and only if $\\sum_{a=1}^{q}K_a^*K_a=I_d$."
    ],
    "thm-qsm-dt-channel-duality": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\Phi:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_e(\\mathbb C)$ be a finite channel as defined in [[def-qsm-dt-channel-definition]]. There is a unique linear map $\\Phi^\\dagger:M_e(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ satisfying the trace-pairing identity\n\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi(X)A)=\\operatorname{Tr}(X\\Phi^\\dagger(A))\\qquad(X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C),\\ A\\in M_e(\\mathbb C)).$$\n\nIf $\\Phi(X)=\\sum_aK_aXK_a^*$, then $\\Phi^\\dagger(A)=\\sum_aK_a^*AK_a$; in particular, $\\Phi^\\dagger$ is completely positive and unital. Composition and finite convex combinations of channels are channels. A limit in the induced operator norm $\\|\\Psi\\|_{\\infty\\to\\infty}:=\\sup_{\\|X\\|_\\infty\\leq1}\\|\\Psi(X)\\|_\\infty$, where $\\|X\\|_\\infty:=\\sup_{\\|v\\|=1}\\|Xv\\|$, of channels $M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_e(\\mathbb C)$ is also a channel. Moreover, a norm limit of completely positive maps of these fixed finite dimensions is completely positive."
    ],
    "thm-qsm-dt-gksl-sufficient": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\mathcal L$ be a finite GKSL generator on $M_d(\\mathbb C)$, $d\\geq1$, with the units and conventions of [[def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition]]. Then $T_t=e^{t\\mathcal L}$ is a finite channel for every $t\\geq0$. The family satisfies $T_0=\\operatorname{id}$, $T_{t+s}=T_t\\circ T_s$, and is norm-continuous. Time is measured in seconds; no negative-time channel or physical bath limit is asserted."
    ],
    "thm-qsm-dt-reduced-unitary": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $H_S=\\mathbb C^d$ and $H_E=\\mathbb C^m$, with $d,m\\geq1$, in fixed ordered orthonormal bases. Let $H_{\\mathrm{tot}}=H_{\\mathrm{tot}}^*$ be a matrix on $H_S\\otimes H_E$ with energy units joules, let $\\hbar>0$ have units joule-seconds, and set $U_t=e^{-itH_{\\mathrm{tot}}/\\hbar}$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ measured in seconds. Let $\\sigma_E\\geq0$ with $\\operatorname{Tr}\\sigma_E=1$, and define\n\n$$\\Phi_t(\\rho):=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\!\\left(U_t(\\rho\\otimes\\sigma_E)U_t^*\\right)\\qquad(\\rho\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)).$$\n\nFor each $t$, $\\Phi_t:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ is a finite quantum channel. The theorem concerns the product preparation $\\rho\\otimes\\sigma_E$; it makes no claim that a correlated initial joint state defines a map of the system marginal alone."
    ],
    "thm-qsm-dt-trace-contraction": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\Phi:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_e(\\mathbb C)$ be a finite channel, and define $\\|X\\|_\\infty=\\sup_{\\|v\\|=1}\\|Xv\\|$ and $\\|X\\|_1=\\operatorname{Tr}\\sqrt{X^*X}$. Its trace dual $\\Phi^\\dagger:M_e(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ from [[thm-qsm-dt-channel-duality]] satisfies\n\n$$\\|\\Phi^\\dagger(A)\\|_\\infty\\leq\\|A\\|_\\infty\\qquad(A\\in M_e(\\mathbb C)).$$ For every $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, the Schrödinger map contracts the trace norm: $$\\|\\Phi(X)\\|_1\\leq\\|X\\|_1.$$\n\nThe second inequality applies to arbitrary complex matrices, not only density matrices."
    ],
    "thm-qsm-dt-gksl-necessary": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $d\\geq1$ and let $T_t:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$, $t\\geq0$ measured in seconds, be a semigroup of finite channels, so $T_0=\\operatorname{id}$ and $T_{t+s}=T_t\\circ T_s$. Assume $t\\mapsto T_t$ is continuous in the induced operator norm on the finite-dimensional space of linear maps $M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$. Then there is a unique linear generator $\\mathcal L$, in ${\\rm s}^{-1}$, with $T_t=e^{t\\mathcal L}$, and $\\mathcal L$ has the finite GKSL form in [[def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition]] with at most $d^2-1$ jump matrices. Conversely, every such finite GKSL generator produces a norm-continuous channel semigroup, as in [[thm-qsm-dt-gksl-sufficient]]."
    ],
    "thm-qsm-dt-pinching-entropy": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $H$ be a complex Hilbert space of finite dimension $d\\geq1$, and let $P_0,\\ldots,P_{r-1}$ be pairwise orthogonal projections, meaning $P_j=P_j^*=P_j^2$ and $P_jP_k=0$ for $j\\ne k$, with $r\\geq1$ and $\\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}P_j=I$. Define the pinching map $\\Delta:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ by $\\Delta(X)=\\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}P_jXP_j$. For every positive semidefinite trace-one matrix $\\rho$, $\\Delta$ is a finite channel and\n\n$$S(\\Delta(\\rho))\\geq S(\\rho),$$\n\nwhere $S(\\rho)=-k_B\\sum_\\lambda\\lambda\\log\\lambda$, the sum is over eigenvalues with multiplicity, $0\\log0=0$, the logarithm is natural, and $k_B>0$ has units joule per kelvin. The theorem concerns only this finite pinching operation; it does not say that every channel increases entropy."
    ],
    "thm-qsm-dt-time-dependent-gksl": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $I=[s_0,s_1]\\subset\\mathbb R$ be a compact time interval in seconds, $d\\geq1$, $H:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ continuous with $H(t)=H(t)^*$ in joules, and $L_a:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ continuous with finite $a=1,\\ldots,m$ in ${\\rm s}^{-1/2}$. At each $t\\in I$, form $\\mathcal L_t$ by [[def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition]]. For every $s\\leq t$ in $I$, the propagator $\\Lambda(t,s)$ solving\n\n$$\\partial_t\\Lambda(t,s)=\\mathcal L_t\\circ\\Lambda(t,s),\\qquad \\Lambda(s,s)=\\operatorname{id},$$\n\nexists uniquely and is a finite channel. No semigroup law is asserted when $t\\mapsto\\mathcal L_t$ varies."
    ],
